WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.320
Even in death, Daenerys Targaryen remains
something of an enigma.

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:09.420
The assassination of Emilia Clarke's icy-haired,
aspiring queen of dragons on HBO's Game of

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.580
Thrones was a particularly shocking moment
on a series filled with them.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.380
On the off-chance that you need a refresher,
here's a bit of background: After Daenerys

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.140
went mad and torched the capital city of King's
Landing, she finally reached the Iron Throne.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.320
But Daenerys never actually took her place
as the ruler of Westeros.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.460
Before she could even so much as take a seat,
her nephew-slash-lover Jon Snow - played by

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.410
Kit Harington - assassinated her for the good
of the realm.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.380
It was a choice that led to his banishment
back to the Night's Watch, bringing Jon's

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.600
story full circle.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.140
Following Daenerys' death, her grieving dragon
Drogon wasted no time torching the Iron Throne

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:46.920
to the ground.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.640
Then, the fiery creature scooped up Dany's
body and flew off to an unknown location.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.460
Since the final Game of Thrones episode aired,
many fans have wondered what exactly happened

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.680
to Daenerys's body after she was killed.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.320
Where did Drogon take her, and what did he
subsequently do to her?

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.960
Now, these mysteries have been somewhat solved,
thanks to the commentary track on the DVD

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.700
release of the show's final season.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.500
According to Entertainment Weekly, showrunners
D.B. Weiss and David Benioff have finally

00:01:13.509 --> 00:01:18.400
revealed what Drogon did with Daenerys' body
- or, at the very least, one big thing he

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.580
didn't do with it.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.640
They've finally debunked the disturbing theory
that Drogon actively ate his mother and master.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.460
You wouldn't do that, would you, Drogon?

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.240
In the commentary track, Emilia Clarke notes
that she would have expected Drogon to simply

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.440
eat the body of his former master, to which
Benioff replied:

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.600
"Drogon's not going to eat you.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:43.560
He's not a cat.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.860
Did you see how gently he was nudging you?"

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:51.340
So that's good to know: Drogon didn't just
fly away with Daenerys' body and dump it somewhere,

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.580
nor did he ravenously devour the remains of
his human "mother."

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:59.740
At the end of the series finale, Samwell Tarly
- played by John Bradley - notes where Drogon

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:00.460
was last seen.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:01.680
"And Drogon?

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:02.960
Any word?"

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.580
"He was last spotted heading east towards
Volan--"

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.600
“Farther away, the better.”

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:12.840
He's cut off, but not before revealing a huge
clue: It sounds like Drogon took Dany to Volantis,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.000
which is where Daenerys and her brother Viserys
spent some time following the assassination

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.110
of their father during Robert's Rebellion.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.960
Benioff took on another theory about Drogon,
but he provides far less closure here.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:28.560
We may never know exactly why Drogon destroyed
the Throne after Daenerys died, but Benioff

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.340
did hint at his intention in relation to Dany's
own desires, saying,

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.900
"If [Dany is] not going to sit on it, no one's
going to sit on it."

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.579
When discussing anything even peripherally
related to Daenerys' death - including what

00:02:40.579 --> 00:02:45.610
happened to her body afterwards - one must
inevitably speak the name of the man who performed

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:47.379
the deed: Jon Snow.

00:02:47.379 --> 00:02:51.900
Daenerys' fiery rampage through the streets
of King's Landing left countless civilians

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.709
dead, and it became abundantly clear that
her ruthlessness knew no bounds - a fact that

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.020
left Jon with a difficult decision to make.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.480
After Peter Dinklage's Tyrion Lannister quits
his post as Daenerys' Hand of the Queen in

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.220
protest, Jon visits him in captivity, and
the two have a serious talk about Jon's next

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:09.720
steps.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.000
Will he turn the other cheek to Dany's shocking
crimes and choose to love her, even though

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.870
she's clearly drunk with power?

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:20.660
Or will he kill her, thus ending the chaos
and destruction she brought to Westeros?

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.100
We all know Jon ultimately chose the latter,
stabbing Daenerys in the chest as the two

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:25.600
embraced.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.580
And according to the Game of Thrones DVD commentary,
Jon didn't know he was going to do the dreadful

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.480
deed until seconds before it happened.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.750
It wasn't his talk with Tyrion that convinced
him to make that grave decision.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:40.900
It was hearing Dany's warped views on power
and privilege that ultimately made him kill

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:41.400
her.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:42.820
As Harrington puts it,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.820
"He doesn't know he's going to betray her
until right at the end.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:51.120
In Jon's head, it's a number of reasons: [Dany]
doesn't factor in anyone else's decisions

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.480
and it also means [Dany] is going to kill
my sisters, so it becomes [my] family vs.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:55.980
her."

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.040
Well, Dany may be dead - but at least she
didn't become dragon chow.

00:04:00.049 --> 00:04:04.709
And it sounds like Emilia Clarke has her own
theories about what ultimately became of Drogon

00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:08.739
and Dany's corpse, judging by what she told
Vanity Fair in November 2019:

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:10.640
"He's just roaming the seas.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:11.640
Crying.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:12.640
Arrrf."

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:14.150
"You think he's somewhere happy?

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:15.520
Flying with the corpse in his mouth?"

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.020
"Yeah 'cuz it's his momma!"

